HTTP response parser
net/http-response.zero.part is the shared source. client-poll.zero.part embeds
it between BEGIN/END HTTP RESPONSE PARSER. The independent native Zero checker
embeds the same fragment; the integrator must refresh both after changes.
Input raw length is at most65535, leaving one terminator byte. Body length is
authoritative: embedded NUL and UTF8 bytes are preserved. No ASCII-body filter
is applied to packet reception. Only legacy fetch sanitizes its raw terminal
view; decoded browser data remains unchanged. fetch wait state740 is separate
from native guest-service state700, and requires a firmware boot handoff.

Only complete HTTP/1.0 or HTTP/1.1 responses are accepted. Status must be
200..599; interim1xx is explicitly unsupported. Header syntax is strict ASCII
with CRLF, token names and no obsolete folded lines. Header block is at most
8192 bytes and128 fields. Content-Length values are decimal, bounded and
duplicate values must agree. Transfer-Encoding permits one chunked field;
Transfer-Encoding plus Content-Length is rejected. Content-Encoding supports
only absent or one identity field; compressed gzip/br/deflate is not decoded.

Chunk sizes, quoted/token extensions, CRLF, terminal zero and trailers are
validated. Chunk lines cap1024 bytes, trailers cap8192 bytes/128fields. Trailer
framing/encoding/MIME/Location overrides are rejected. No trailing bytes are
accepted after chunk completion. The separate decoded buffer never exceeds
65535 bytes. 204 must have no body and no CL/TE;304 must have no body and can
carry representation Content-Length or Transfer-Encoding metadata. Duplicate
Content-Type/Location fields, empty values or values over255 bytes fail.
Ordinary responses without CL/TE consume the closed-stream body. A declared
length mismatch fails rather than presenting a truncated page. Every new
request clears previous body length, status, MIME and redirect metadata.

Requests and limitations
Request format HOST[:PORT][/PATH], optionally prefixed http://. IPv4 literals
bypass DNS. Names use bounded ASCII DNS labels. Userinfo, IPv6, IDNA and TLS
are unsupported; HTTPS is rejected, never silently downgraded. Requests use
HTTP/1.0, Connection:close, Accept-Encoding:identity and User-Agent:Zero/0.13.
The client waits for server close, so persistent peers ignoring close time
out. Redirect policy belongs to the browser and must reuse URL validation.
Charset conversion, image codecs, JavaScript, CSS and TLS are not transport
features. UTF8 body preservation does not imply all Unicode glyphs render.

IPv4 lengths/checksum/nonfragmentation, UDP checksum when present, DNS
source/id/question/bounded records, TCP tuple/checksum/sequence/overlap and
retries are checked. DHCP, DNS and HTTP are unauthenticated protocols. This
is a bounded first transport, not a claim of modern all-site compatibility.
